---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/sendlane-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/sendlane-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/sendlane-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Did the acquisition shut down Sendlane?",
    "answer": "The cited February 2026 announcement establishes the acquisition, not a shutdown date or an automatic account transfer. Obtain the current plan for your account from its support contact."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should be handed over?",
    "answer": "Record account access, billing and support continuity alongside templates, approvals, forms, permission records, integrations, scheduled campaigns and recovery queues. Give each item an accountable owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can we keep the current setup?",
    "answer": "Yes, if the account-specific support and product plan meets your needs. Compare staying, a documented supplier transition and independent procurement using the same acceptance requirements."
  },
  {
    "question": "When can we enable the new journey?",
    "answer": "After creative approval, permission reconciliation, event and purchase-exit checks, and queue ownership are accepted. Reconcile sends before any rollback; elapsed time alone is insufficient."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Sendlane alternatives: turn an acquisition review into an accountable handover</h1>
<h2>Distinguish the announcement from your account plan</h2>
<p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy’s February 2, 2026 announcement</a> says it acquired Sendlane, describing Sendlane’s email and SMS offering and hands-on support. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> presents a unified-platform direction. The announcement does not establish a shutdown date or automatic transfer of your account, assets or permissions. The service-first approach is a positive reason to evaluate continuity before deciding to leave.</p>
<h2>Request an account-specific continuity plan</h2>
<p>Ask the current account contact to confirm login and administrator access, billing entity, renewal terms, export availability, support route and who owns active campaigns. Request written answers about any proposed product or account transition, including affected features, required action and fallback access. Record unanswered items with an owner. Compare three concrete options: keep the supported setup, accept a documented transition, or procure an independent replacement. Do not migrate solely because ownership changed.</p>
<h2>Make creative and service work visible</h2>
<p>Create a handover register for templates, images, reusable blocks, copy approvals, forms, campaign drafts, scheduled sends and automation branches. Include whoever currently builds campaigns or repairs feeds. Save available originals and map each asset to a rebuilt version, an explicit retirement or a retained source. Rebuild an ordinary campaign and a cart message in the receiving editor; check merge fields, mobile layout, currency, links, unsubscribe behavior and whether the marketer can edit the result without the original implementer.</p>
<h2>Reconcile records without silently resubscribing people</h2>
<p>Export available contact and event identifiers, preferences, consent evidence, unsubscribe history, suppressions and custom fields. Reconcile totals by sendable, suppressed and unresolved state rather than raw import count. Identify forms, API callers and Shopify syncs that can overwrite status. Test a later profile update against an earlier unsubscribe and require the exclusion to survive. Acquisition communications do not confer new permission, and a successful import does not demonstrate permission parity.</p>
<h2>Use a service handover rehearsal</h2>
<p>The following exercises are proposals, not performed tests. Have the receiving operator change a cart template, repair a missing product image and route a customer reply to the correct support owner. Retain an approval record and escalation contact for each task. Compare response responsibilities and implementation work in the incumbent continuity plan and replacement quote. If a service task disappears, budget for an employee or agency to own it before accepting a cheaper software price.</p>
<h2>Accept the recovery journey before moving the queue</h2>
<p>Create a mobile cart, return through its link, change an item and purchase before a pending reminder. Replay the event and verify one eligible journey with no purchaser reminder. Test an unsubscribe while queued and overlapping welcome and cart messages. Inventory old scheduled campaigns and recovery queues; assign one sending owner per journey, pause the superseded queue and reconcile already-sent messages before enabling the new one. Preserve rollback configuration, but reconcile sends before reactivation.</p>
<h2>Keep the merchandising edge cases</h2>
<p>For an out-of-stock variant, suppress the stale offer; for a bundle, verify component availability. For gift sizing, preserve the useful guide and reply destination. For shipping shock, check destination charges; for financing abandonment, retain accurate payment-help content. For minimum-order quantities, show the actual threshold. Test stacked-code exclusions and repeat-customer eligibility in the rebuilt creative. These cases preserve useful baseline coverage without repeating dozens of AOV and minute permutations. Choose timing, frequency and control-group size from actual traffic and the decision being measured.</p>
<h2>Compare procurement against continuity</h2>
<p>Use <a href="/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative">the Klaviyo evaluation</a> for an event and segmentation procurement brief or <a href="/alternatives/omnisend-alternative">the Omnisend evaluation</a> for an ecommerce workflow brief. Confirm current capabilities and commercial scope in a demonstration. Neither is a ranked winner here. Compare creative reconstruction, integration effort, service ownership, ongoing fees and continuity risk alongside the current account plan; an additional lifecycle layer is not a prerequisite.</p>
<h2>Cost the retained offer before approving it</h2>
<p>Illustrative fixed-goods-cost model: a $42 order at 51% gross margin has $20.58 goods cost and $21.42 gross profit. A 17% selling-price discount reduces revenue to $34.86 and gross profit to $14.28, a $7.14 reduction. Add messaging, shipping, refunds and platform costs separately. Measure incremental contribution with a defined eligible control and sufficient observations; vendor-attributed revenue is not proof of incremental profit.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
